which I think is a more obvious leap in my mind&nbsp;
than say messaging. What I've observed is say in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.360 --> 00:14:36.640
the media landscape, well the media landscape and&nbsp;
the social media platform landscapes have collided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.640 --> 00:14:43.280
in such a major way in the last five to 10 years&nbsp;
where you have Amazon Studios, you've got Netflix,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.280 --> 00:14:50.560
you've got messaging, and then video and so on&nbsp;
that are seemingly all being pursued on some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.560 --> 00:14:55.280
level by a lot of these large platforms. In the&nbsp;
forms of, say WhatsApp or whatever it might be.&nbsp;

00:14:55.280 --> 00:15:00.000
How do you choose what to engage in&nbsp;
next? You said some people might say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:04.840
"I'm unfocused," I don't consider you&nbsp;
unfocused, but there are sometimes hidden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.840 --> 00:15:11.904
or unspoken rationales or logics behind. How do&nbsp;
you think about what you're going to do next?&nbsp;

00:15:11.904 --> 00:15:16.560
Matt Mullenweg: I do think about it for a long&nbsp;
time. We've been thinking about messaging and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.560 --> 00:15:23.880
actually making investments in this space for four&nbsp;
or five years. I think a lot about environment and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.880 --> 00:15:31.040
incentives. The reason there used to be these&nbsp;
multi-messaging apps 15 years ago and they all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.040 --> 00:15:37.200
stopped working was the networks all blocked&nbsp;
them. There is a political environment now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.200 --> 00:15:43.080
which I think is more conducive to being more&nbsp;
customer and user-centric. This is our data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.080 --> 00:15:46.440
this is our messages, it's all secure,&nbsp;
it's not breaking security or anything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.440 --> 00:15:50.120
so why shouldn't we be able to run this.
Tim Ferriss: Can you give an example of a network&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.120 --> 00:15:55.800
blocking these multi-message tools in the past?
Matt Mullenweg: Yeah, it happened last week.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.800 --> 00:16:01.120
There was another one called Beeper that&nbsp;
supported iMessage and Apple decided to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.120 --> 00:16:06.400
just shut it down. They broke it all and Beeper&nbsp;
actually charged its users. They had to refund&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.400 --> 00:16:12.760
everyone. It's also more subtle things they&nbsp;
could do. They could just subtly degrade if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.760 --> 00:16:16.880
they make it so your messages don't go through&nbsp;
five percent of the time, it's not blocking you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.440 --> 00:16:19.680
but you're going to stop using it.
Tim Ferriss: It's like throttling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.680 --> 00:16:24.344
your hotel speed on Wi-Fi. So you upgrade to&nbsp;
the premium, you're like, "Okay, all right."&nbsp;

00:16:24.344 --> 00:16:26.600
Matt Mullenweg: They don't need to block&nbsp;
you, which might draw attention. They can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.600 --> 00:16:29.840
just make you a little wobbly.
Tim Ferriss: More painful.&nbsp;

00:16:29.840 --> 00:16:33.400
Matt Mullenweg: It doesn't take a lot of friction&nbsp;
for people to move away, especially in messaging.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.400 --> 00:16:38.240
The regulatory framework, both with the&nbsp;
EU doing a lot of sometimes misguided,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.240 --> 00:16:42.640
but also sometimes really smart, they have&nbsp;
an act coming in called the DMA that requires&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.640 --> 00:16:46.800
some interop between messaging services. We&nbsp;
will talk about USB-C, which I'm very excited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.800 --> 00:16:52.160
about. Thank you EU, for forcing Apple to&nbsp;
finally drop lightning and give us USB-C.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.160 --> 00:16:57.200
Then in the US I think there is bipartisan, this&nbsp;
I actually don't agree with, but it is a reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.200 --> 00:17:03.520
some extra scrutiny on big tech. I think it's&nbsp;
actually good for them. Again, what are their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.520 --> 00:17:09.480
incentives? I think it's actually really good for&nbsp;
them to show that they're open right now. I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.480 --> 00:17:15.280
want to fight these folks. They've got more&nbsp;
money than most countries. They could squash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.280 --> 00:17:20.680
us like a bug if they really, really wanted&nbsp;
to, but we're always doing things open source,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.680 --> 00:17:22.760
user-centric, and so it's —
Tim Ferriss: It'd be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:22.760 --> 00:17:25.520
bad look for them to try to squash.
Matt Mullenweg: If you have the people on your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.520 --> 00:17:30.920
side, I feel like that's what truly matters in&nbsp;
long-term. People are what, short-term, lobbying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.920 --> 00:17:38.320
et cetera, but long-term in the US, functioning&nbsp;
democracy, politics is accountable to its people.&nbsp;

00:17:38.320 --> 00:17:43.560
Tim Ferriss: If we come back to what you said and&nbsp;
the whole point of this format is scaffolding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.560 --> 00:17:48.480
and then we can deviate. We're deviating right&nbsp;
now. People may have noticed. Talking about a,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.480 --> 00:17:55.240
let's just say next, next gen or digital Berkshire&nbsp;
Hathaway. Berkshire Hathaway could have, well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.240 --> 00:17:57.920
originally textiles, but they&nbsp;
could have insurance, then they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.920 --> 00:18:00.347
could have something that is —
Matt Mullenweg: Chocolates.&nbsp;

00:18:00.347 --> 00:18:07.520
Tim Ferriss: Chocolates, right? See's Candies,&nbsp;
things that are completely unrelated on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.520 --> 00:18:15.520
face-value business level. They're not integrated&nbsp;
in the way that a CMS or having a gajillion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.520 --> 00:18:20.800
blogs and websites running on your platform&nbsp;
would combine very easily with WooCommerce,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.800 --> 00:18:27.280
right? Those two pair very nicely. Are you&nbsp;
thinking about, say in the case of texts.com&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.280 --> 00:18:32.680
that is a standalone in the same way that some of&nbsp;
these Berkshire Hathaway might be a standalone?&nbsp;

00:18:32.680 --> 00:18:37.440
How do you think about building and acquiring in&nbsp;
that way? Are they standalones? How much do they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.440 --> 00:18:45.360
need to help each other or not? In the case of&nbsp;
Berkshire, right, the insurance premiums and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.360 --> 00:18:52.600
on, as I understand it, provide a huge bolus of&nbsp;
cash for all sorts of other purposes. The capital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.600 --> 00:18:59.720
can be utilized across the family, in a sense.&nbsp;
How do you think about, because there's so many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:59.720 --> 00:19:05.640
different ways you could rank order the priorities&nbsp;
when looking at potential acquisitions, is it just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.640 --> 00:19:13.344
like customer pain point, converging trend lines&nbsp;
in terms of regulation and public sentiment and —&nbsp;

00:19:13.344 --> 00:19:16.920
Matt Mullenweg: Which Berkshire navigates&nbsp;
beautifully in highly regulated industries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:16.920 --> 00:19:23.960
including insurance, railways. How I think&nbsp;
about it, I like to study these people. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:23.960 --> 00:19:27.787
know you love doing that too. Study high&nbsp;
performers, that's your thing, right?&nbsp;

00:19:27.787 --> 00:19:30.160
Tim Ferriss: I do that sometimes.&nbsp;
Yeah, occasionally do that.&nbsp;

00:19:30.160 --> 00:19:35.280
Matt Mullenweg: I mean, Charlie Munger, rest&nbsp;
in peace, 99 years old. Warren Buffett, I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:35.280 --> 00:19:41.120
know exactly. I think up there as well. I try to&nbsp;
think if Warren Buffett and Charlie Munger were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.120 --> 00:19:47.800
hackers in their 30s or 40s today, what would they&nbsp;
build versus what they built in their time with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:47.800 --> 00:19:52.680
the opportunities in technology afforded? I like&nbsp;
to think they do some open source because it's,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.680 --> 00:20:00.960
obviously, the future. I like the Berkshire for&nbsp;
atoms companies versus — atoms versus bits — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:00.960 --> 00:20:04.424
in the industries, they're in —
Tim Ferriss: A-T-O-M-S.&nbsp;

00:20:04.424 --> 00:20:08.640
Matt Mullenweg: I think there would be a lot of&nbsp;
coordination costs and they try to optimize for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.640 --> 00:20:14.240
giving the companies under them as much autonomy&nbsp;
as possible. They find great leaders and they also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:14.240 --> 00:20:17.760
try to find great businesses. I think Warren&nbsp;
Buffett said something like, "We try to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:17.760 --> 00:20:22.640
a business a monkey could run, because someday&nbsp;
they will," something like that. What business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:22.640 --> 00:20:27.520
is so good, it would survive even bad leadership.&nbsp;
What we do with a digital version of that is we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:27.520 --> 00:20:34.080
lower coordination costs between the different&nbsp;
products by open source. They don't need, like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.080 --> 00:20:39.240
For WooCommerce, to build on top of WordPress.&nbsp;
It doesn't need a meeting, doesn't need to talk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:39.240 --> 00:20:43.560
doesn't even to know the people developing&nbsp;
WordPress. There's open APIs, open source,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:43.560 --> 00:20:47.320
there's a plugin framework, et cetera. That&nbsp;
removes a lot of the coordination costs that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:47.320 --> 00:20:50.640
you normally get in a multi division company.
Tim Ferriss: Now when you say coordination costs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:50.640 --> 00:20:54.960
are you talking about people internally, say,&nbsp;
full-time employees? Or are you talking about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:54.960 --> 00:21:01.240
communities that surround some of these things?
Matt Mullenweg: In proprietary software,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:01.240 --> 00:21:09.680
if you want to integrate with something, like if I&nbsp;
wanted to add a new feature to Mac OS or something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:09.680 --> 00:21:15.960
like that, I can't do that. Now, they have APIs,&nbsp;
they have operating systems. Smart companies like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:15.960 --> 00:21:20.240
Slack or Shopify will create marketplaces that&nbsp;
you can extend them. However, you're subject&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:20.240 --> 00:21:24.680
to their terms so they can change their mind.&nbsp;
Remember Twitter used to have all those clients,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:24.680 --> 00:21:28.660
or Facebook did too. They were like, "Oh, we&nbsp;
don't want this anymore. You're all kicked off."&nbsp;

00:21:28.660 --> 00:21:30.640
Tim Ferriss: Rough Tuesday.
Matt Mullenweg: Don't build on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:30.640 --> 00:21:35.360
proprietary platforms. They can pull the rug and&nbsp;
will pull the rug at any point. In open source,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:35.360 --> 00:21:39.480
one, the rug can't be pulled. Two,&nbsp;
typically they're ultra pluggable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:39.480 --> 00:21:43.280
You can really change every line of WordPress.
Tim Ferriss: Not to interrupt, but would you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:43.280 --> 00:21:49.980
mind giving a real world example of what this&nbsp;
lowered integration cost looks like in practice?&nbsp;

00:21:49.980 --> 00:21:52.720
Matt Mullenweg: Yeah. Let's talk about a&nbsp;
company like Salesforce, which has done a ton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:52.720 --> 00:21:58.960
of acquisitions. One criticism of a Salesforce&nbsp;
or Cisco or even Google: sometimes their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:58.960 --> 00:22:04.640
products don't integrate with each other as well.&nbsp;
Sometimes even external things do. So even with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:04.640 --> 00:22:09.080
the best acquisitions, and most acquisitions fail&nbsp;
by the way, even when you do a really good one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:09.080 --> 00:22:14.520
maybe say YouTube, sometimes the integrations&nbsp;
aren't as strong. Remember when YouTube tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:14.520 --> 00:22:16.440
to do the Google+ thing?
Tim Ferriss: I do.&nbsp;

00:22:16.440 --> 00:22:21.520
Matt Mullenweg: Every single division in Google&nbsp;
was incentivized by how much adoption Google+&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.520 --> 00:22:26.120
got. They really tried to push it into&nbsp;
everything. A lot of coordination costs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:26.120 --> 00:22:29.960
maybe not as responsive to users. That was&nbsp;
ultimately an unsuccessful push. Google's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:29.960 --> 00:22:33.720
another example. All the messaging platforms&nbsp;
they have, you need text just to work with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:33.720 --> 00:22:39.080
five messaging platforms at Google. There's&nbsp;
examples like that where you get duplication,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:39.080 --> 00:22:43.120
you get different incentives of executives, maybe&nbsp;
they're more rewarded for launching new things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:43.120 --> 00:22:49.040
versus integrating things. Those are coordination&nbsp;
costs. This comes up in economics terms where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:49.720 --> 00:22:54.480
why shouldn't everyone just be a freelancer?
Well, there's some coordination costs there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:54.480 --> 00:22:59.760
It's nice to have people employed full-time by a&nbsp;
company. Then you don't need to rehire them every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:59.760 --> 00:23:03.920
time. They're not going to be poached by someone&nbsp;
else. Maybe you have a gap in the gig and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:03.920 --> 00:23:09.920
just take another gig, that sort of thing. I feel&nbsp;
like for us, the common platform of open source,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:09.920 --> 00:23:15.120
particularly WordPress, allows us to plug things&nbsp;
in and do acquisitions in a way that is more set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:15.120 --> 00:23:21.560
up for success. We have a set of products that&nbsp;
run directly on WordPress or get distribution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:21.560 --> 00:23:27.240
from WordPress and Tumblr. That's, I would&nbsp;
say, our core area. There are some which are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:27.240 --> 00:23:31.800
I would call, philosophically adjacent.&nbsp;
It's the same philosophy. Day One is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:31.800 --> 00:23:36.640
a great example. Day One doesn't share&nbsp;
any technology with Tumblr or WordPress,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:36.640 --> 00:23:41.720
but it's a fully encrypted local journaling&nbsp;
app. Journaling is another word for blogging.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:41.720 --> 00:23:48.360
You can use it like I do as a local blog. I&nbsp;
posted every day for the past 200 or 300 days.&nbsp;

00:23:48.360 --> 00:23:52.520
Tim Ferriss: What does "local blog" mean?&nbsp;
To the untrained ear, they'd be like, "Wait,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:52.520 --> 00:23:56.560
so I have a blog that I'm publishing on&nbsp;
my own computer? How do people see it?"&nbsp;

00:23:56.560 --> 00:24:01.120
Matt Mullenweg: Well, this is why people usually&nbsp;
call it a journal. But when you think about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:01.120 --> 00:24:07.600
like a "notes" app, like the Notes app, I&nbsp;
don't know, typically things are undated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:07.600 --> 00:24:13.640
right? The metadata associated with them is&nbsp;
somewhat loose. Often the list is ordered by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:13.640 --> 00:24:20.720
most recently modified. That's kind of a UI. In&nbsp;
a blog, it's reverse chronological. We have a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:20.720 --> 00:24:25.920
of metadata associated. Day One attaches a date,&nbsp;
location, we can store the weather when you post&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:25.920 --> 00:24:29.920
it. All these different things that kind of make&nbsp;
it a bit richer. When it's local, we usually call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:29.920 --> 00:24:34.920
it a journal, just because that's the concept.&nbsp;
Apple just launched a built-in journaling app.&nbsp;

00:24:34.920 --> 00:24:39.760
I call it a blog because fundamentally if you&nbsp;
kind of look at those principles, it's got all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:39.760 --> 00:24:44.560
the same ingredients as you post a blog reverse&nbsp;
chronological. Now why do I really like it? I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:44.560 --> 00:24:49.520
prefer dated entries because I'm usually taking&nbsp;
notes each day. Kind of like Benjamin Franklin. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:49.520 --> 00:24:53.520
would log everything he does every day. I do that&nbsp;
as well. I love the search, I love tagging, I love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:53.520 --> 00:24:58.600
all those metadata things. Help me find stuff. You&nbsp;
can also interlink the notes, which is actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:58.600 --> 00:25:05.467
pretty cool. Not unlike a Roam Research or some of&nbsp;
these other — Obsidian. You can interlink things.&nbsp;

00:25:05.467 --> 00:25:09.720
Tim Ferriss: Okay, so we took a&nbsp;
little side alley if we come back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:09.720 --> 00:25:11.840
to things that you're excited about.
Matt Mullenweg: I didn't actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:11.840 --> 00:25:13.640
answer your question on messaging.
Tim Ferriss: You did not, yes.&nbsp;

00:25:13.640 --> 00:25:19.880
Matt Mullenweg: So why messaging? It is not&nbsp;
built on top of WordPress and it's not part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:19.880 --> 00:25:28.800
of our publishing kind of thing, but I do believe&nbsp;
it is fundamental human right to have private and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:28.800 --> 00:25:34.800
hopefully in the future, open source messaging.&nbsp;
Again, I only want to work on things I feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:34.800 --> 00:25:42.200
like I can work on potentially the rest of my&nbsp;
life. So publishing commerce and messaging,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:42.200 --> 00:25:44.664
that covers a lot of human activity.
Tim Ferriss: It does.&nbsp;

00:25:44.664 --> 00:25:48.400
Matt Mullenweg: And if you have those things&nbsp;
truly free, I think you have a free society.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:48.400 --> 00:25:53.880
And that's also exciting to me because how do&nbsp;
we help bend the long arc towards more freedom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:53.880 --> 00:26:01.440
more liberty across the world? And technology does&nbsp;
that better than, I think, any sort of diplomacy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:01.440 --> 00:26:06.480
or anything else that at least I could work on.
Tim Ferriss: Are there any other people or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:06.480 --> 00:26:12.480
companies that stand out as being&nbsp;
aligned or philosophically adjacent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:12.480 --> 00:26:15.000
with the ethos you're describing?
Matt Mullenweg: Oh, yeah. Well, one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:15.000 --> 00:26:20.360
there's a lot more open source companies now.&nbsp;
GitLab is a really great one led by Sid. They're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:20.360 --> 00:26:24.920
actually even more open than we are. They publish&nbsp;
like everything and they're a public company now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:24.920 --> 00:26:29.080
like nine or $10 billion. So that's pretty&nbsp;
cool to have those examples. I think Element,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:29.080 --> 00:26:32.760
which is built on the Matrix ecosystem,&nbsp;
so open source messaging, actually the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:32.760 --> 00:26:37.040
competitor to text Beeper, really awesome&nbsp;
company, I think philosophically very aligned.&nbsp;

00:26:37.040 --> 00:26:42.360
So what's cool is more and more of this is&nbsp;
happening. Also, there's a fun trend where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:42.360 --> 00:26:47.400
sometimes people who did proprietary companies&nbsp;
and then made a ton of money off them, what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:47.400 --> 00:26:54.040
they do next is often open source. So Jack Dorsey&nbsp;
made him a ton of money off Twitter, Square. One,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:54.040 --> 00:26:59.320
he's taken Square to more of a crypto direction.&nbsp;
He wants to enable that. And two, what's he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:59.320 --> 00:27:04.720
funding? Something called Nostr and Bluesky, which&nbsp;
are two competing open source Twitters, which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:04.720 --> 00:27:10.720
really cool. Brian Acton, co-founder of WhatsApp.&nbsp;
What's he doing today? He's running Signal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:10.720 --> 00:27:15.040
which is an open source nonprofit messaging app,&nbsp;
which is amazing. Signal, very philosophically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:15.040 --> 00:27:20.627
aligned. So Wikipedia, Mozilla, there's a&nbsp;
lot out there both for-profit and nonprofit.&nbsp;

00:27:20.627 --> 00:27:25.480
Tim Ferriss: Based on the very little I know, and&nbsp;
you track this type of thing much more than I do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:25.480 --> 00:27:31.280
but in terms of number of users per&nbsp;
full-time employee pre acquisition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:31.280 --> 00:27:37.067
how would you place WhatsApp? I mean, it's —
Matt Mullenweg: The messaging apps are tops.&nbsp;

00:27:37.067 --> 00:27:38.760
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Matt Mullenweg: I think Instagram&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:38.760 --> 00:27:45.480
was a pretty good size with 13 or 14 when they&nbsp;
sold. Telegram's actually pretty amazing today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:45.480 --> 00:27:50.240
Signal is pretty small team. I'm not sure the&nbsp;
size of the WhatsApp team now, but they were very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:50.240 --> 00:27:54.600
very small when they were acquired. It's actually&nbsp;
pretty incredible because messaging — there's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:54.600 --> 00:28:01.600
really any user support. It's all self-serve.&nbsp;
And so those businesses can scale quite a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:01.600 --> 00:28:06.000
with very few people and it also attracts&nbsp;
really amazing engineer. The Texts team is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:06.840 --> 00:28:14.480
incredible. And so their aspiration is to remain&nbsp;
a sub-20, sub-30 team, even as they grow to tens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:14.480 --> 00:28:19.000
of millions or hundreds of millions of users.
Tim Ferriss: So how do you, as the buyer first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:19.000 --> 00:28:29.360
resort, hopefully the aspiring buyer, first&nbsp;
resort, Berkshire Hathaway, known as Automattic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:29.360 --> 00:28:37.260
how do you find these various companies or threads&nbsp;
to pull on or how do those people find you?&nbsp;

00:28:37.260 --> 00:28:40.040
Matt Mullenweg: Oh, people do reach out&nbsp;
sometimes, which is always nice. But yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:40.040 --> 00:28:45.200
I guess fundamentally it's usually just driven by&nbsp;
me as a user. I'm always trying out new products,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:45.200 --> 00:28:50.040
friends recommend it. My colleagues actually,&nbsp;
of the people we employ, they tend to be very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:50.040 --> 00:28:54.960
early adopters and very digitally savvy. So&nbsp;
I mean, that's why we launched Bitcoin in&nbsp;&nbsp;

